WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:23.080
Okay, so now we're going to go over to
Christopher Quill. Chris is now going to
show you on the computer how you calculate
your distributions for initially the S&P
500, but you can use this for any asset.
So take it away, Chris.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:59.980
So in this example, we're going to look at
a day trading strategy for the S&P 500
over a period of roughly 50 years. By
calculating these daily returns, we should
get a good idea of how the S&P 500

00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:29.960
the underlying asset behaves behaves on a
daily basis. So we're going to start by
going to Yahoo Finance and obtaining the
data. So finance.yahoo.com. And then in
the ticker box, we type S&P and we select
the first drop down item, S&P 500. And
this gives us a summary phrase

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:59.940
page of the S&P 500 with Yahoo Finance's
last available price for the asset. But
we're going to go to historical prices on
the left hand side and update the
historical price table by editing the
inputs given at the top here. So we're
looking for data from between the 3rd of
January 1962 to the 9th of November 2012
on a daily frequency.

00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:08.800
So once we've set our parameters, we
update the table by clicking Get Prices
and we scroll to the bottom of the page
and click Download to Spreadsheet.

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:20.400
At this stage, you can select to save the
spreadsheet to your hard drive. Or as
we're going to do, you can just open the
spreadsheet straight away and save it once
it's open.

00:02:22.940 --> 00:02:36.160
With the spreadsheet open, we're just
going to go to File, Save As. We're going
to save it as S&P 500 Returns
Distribution.

00:02:37.220 --> 00:02:43.020
And we'll save it as an Excel workbook.
And I'm going to save it on my desktop so
it's easy to find.

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:57.800
So the first thing that we're going to do
is adjust this date column so we can see
the dates.

00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:02.500
So we just click on the column
intersection and adjust the width of the
column like that by clicking and dragging.

00:03:03.320 --> 00:03:09.440
So this is all the price data for the S&P
500 from the daily price data from the
period that we've selected.

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:18.520
So in our example, we're only going to use
the open and closing prices to calculate
daily returns.

00:03:19.820 --> 00:03:27.540
So let's go ahead and do that by heading
up column H with returns in cell H1.

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:37.780
And then in H2, type our returns formula,
which is brackets, closing price for that
day minus the opening price for that day,

00:03:37.900 --> 00:03:43.240
divided by the opening price, which gets
it in terms of a percentage return for
that day.

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:53.060
And then we want to apply the formula that
we've just put into cell H2 down the whole
returns column.

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:57.580
So a quick way to do that is click
anywhere in the data from column A to G.

00:03:58.280 --> 00:04:04.180
So cell G2, for example, and press CTRL
and down to navigate quickly to the bottom
of the spreadsheet.

00:04:04.840 --> 00:04:09.980
And then find the last cell in column H.

00:04:10.600 --> 00:04:13.220
So that is H12804.

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:21.680
And then we press CTRL, shift and up to
quickly select all the return cells that
we want to implement our formula into.

00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:28.800
And then with that selected, we just press
CTRL, D to copy that formula down from
cell H2 to the rest of the column.

00:04:32.840 --> 00:04:38.720
So now we've got our returns, we want to
be able to analyse them and interpret them
in terms of a distribution.

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:47.820
So by that I mean we want to be able to
understand how many of these daily
returns, S&P 500 daily returns,

00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:50.640
lie within specific ranges that we set.

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:55.860
And we're going to set those ranges by
creating intervals in the data.

00:04:56.700 --> 00:05:02.660
So in column J, cell J1, head up the
column with intervals.

00:05:05.080 --> 00:05:05.440
Okay.

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:14.680
And then what we want to do is come up
with a reasonable set of intervals for
trading the S&P 500 daily.

00:05:15.540 --> 00:05:23.460
So a lower bound for the ranges we're
interested in might be minus 2%, for
example, because we're only looking at
daily data.

00:05:24.140 --> 00:05:30.960
So looking at anything with too wider
range would be pointless because it
wouldn't really show us the core of our
data set.

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:37.040
So we'll start with minus 2%, so minus
0.02, we enter into cell J2.

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:42.740
And then we'll go up in increments of half
a percent.

00:05:42.740 --> 00:05:54.720
So in the next cell, J3, we type 0.0.015,
press enter.

00:05:55.420 --> 00:06:02.440
And to quickly use this interval that
we've set and copy it down to an upper
bound of plus 2%,

00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:11.740
we select those two cells, J2 and J3, and
then select the small black box in the
bottom right-hand corner of the selection

00:06:11.740 --> 00:06:22.040
and drag it down to cell J10, which gives
us plus 0.02, which is plus 2%.

00:06:22.040 --> 00:06:39.280
So we're going to use these intervals to
create a distribution table to see the
frequency of our daily returns that land
within these intervals.

00:06:40.560 --> 00:06:48.800
So to do that, we have to go to data on
the ribbon and data analysis on the
right-hand side.

00:06:48.800 --> 00:06:58.580
If you don't have this at this point,
there's a guide of how you obtain this
data analysis option in the accompanying
PDF.

00:06:59.580 --> 00:07:04.420
And you do it by installing an add-in to
Excel called Analysis Toolpack.

00:07:05.500 --> 00:07:07.340
So we go ahead and click on Data Analysis.

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:10.940
And we want to create a histogram data.

00:07:11.500 --> 00:07:13.600
So that's basically a frequency table.

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:16.200
And press OK.

00:07:16.200 --> 00:07:22.760
And we're going to create this table from
our returns data.

00:07:23.500 --> 00:07:31.860
So we select in the input range, cell H2,
and press Ctrl-Shift and down to quickly
select that whole returns column.

00:07:32.620 --> 00:07:40.520
The bin range is just the intervals that
we want to define our ranges with, that
we're interested in interpreting our
returns within.

00:07:40.580 --> 00:07:49.360
So our bin range is just our intervals
that we created in column J, from J2,
Ctrl-Shift down, select to J10.

00:07:51.040 --> 00:07:57.100
Now, we want to display this histogram
data in this spreadsheet that we're
working in, rather than a new worksheet.

00:07:57.880 --> 00:08:07.480
So in output options, we select output
range, and you can see this throws you
back to the input range box, which we've
already filled in with our returns data.

00:08:07.540 --> 00:08:15.320
So we need to be careful to click off that
into output range and select whereabouts
in the spreadsheet we want this frequency
table to be.

00:08:15.440 --> 00:08:27.320
So we're going to use cell L2, and we're
going to use cell L2, and so we click L2
and press OK, and that gives us a tabular
format of a histogram.

00:08:27.700 --> 00:08:49.040
So basically, a frequency table, and what
this essentially means is, if we take the
first row, for example, in this table,
this means that 285 days, the S&P 500
yields a return of less than or equal to
minus 2%.

00:08:49.040 --> 00:08:58.160
And the next row, and the next row, 345
days, yields a return of between minus 2%
and minus 1.5%.

00:08:58.160 --> 00:08:59.960
OK, and that's how the table works.

00:09:00.040 --> 00:09:07.780
So if you go to the bottom, eventually you
get to 307 days, lie greater than 2%.

00:09:07.780 --> 00:09:22.500
OK, so what would be useful is if we
interpreted this data visually, so we
create a graph from this data.

00:09:23.020 --> 00:09:36.200
So to do that, we just select the data,
L3, click and drag to M12 to select that
data, and go to Insert on the ribbon,
Column, and the first 2D Column Chart
option.

00:09:36.960 --> 00:09:39.460
Click that, and that gives us a histogram
of our data.

00:09:40.380 --> 00:09:49.080
OK, so at the moment, what we can see is
that clearly more returns on our
distribution lie close to zero.

00:09:49.720 --> 00:10:00.900
OK, so that's what we can tell so far, and
that a lot less, it sort of tails off, and
it looks something like a bell-shaped
curve, a bit like a, well, a lot like a
normal distribution at the moment,

00:10:03.200 --> 00:10:08.760
where there's a lot less frequency of
returns, where there's a lot less
frequency of returns in the tails, than in
the centre.

00:10:10.940 --> 00:10:17.540
OK, so we should tidy this graph up, and
make it a little bit more easy to
interpret.

00:10:17.740 --> 00:10:21.080
I'm just going to go ahead and zoom out of
the spreadsheet a little bit.

00:10:21.440 --> 00:10:26.460
To do that easily, you can just hold CTRL
and then use your mouse wheel to zoom in
and out of the spreadsheet.

00:10:26.980 --> 00:10:41.180
So, we're going to replace this horizontal
x-axis with some more appropriate labels,
so we can understand the ranges that our
frequency of data lie within.

00:10:42.180 --> 00:10:47.300
So, to do that, let's create a range
column in column N.

00:10:47.800 --> 00:10:49.780
So, in N2, we'll type range.

00:10:50.760 --> 00:10:57.160
And in N3, we'll type the appropriate
range that applies to this bin value, and
so on.

00:10:57.280 --> 00:11:03.040
And we'll do that in all of these cells,
from N3 to N12.

00:11:03.460 --> 00:11:12.880
Before we do that, though, because we're
going to type it in as text, we want to
format these cells, so they know that
these cells are going to be displayed as
text, or understood as text.

00:11:12.960 --> 00:11:17.260
So, we select all those cells, and
right-click within the selection, and
click Format Cells.

00:11:19.140 --> 00:11:29.100
And we then want to change the category to
text, and press OK.

00:11:29.960 --> 00:11:47.600
Okay, so we'll start with the first row,
and we'll call this range less than minus
2%, and then the next range will be minus
2% to minus 1.5%, and so on.

00:11:49.140 --> 00:12:04.140
So, we're just using the bin values as a
reference to complete this table, this
column, rather.

00:12:08.140 --> 00:12:18.600
So, it's a little bit tedious, but it's a
good thing to do, so you can actually
understand your data graphically with the
correct ranges defined.

00:12:19.140 --> 00:12:39.120
And in the last cell, in cell N12, we're
going to type greater than 2%.

00:12:39.120 --> 00:12:46.760
Okay, and let's adjust this column width,
column N, so it can include all that text,
and it doesn't overlap into another
column.

00:12:48.160 --> 00:12:56.540
Okay, so now we want to change the
horizontal axis labels to the ranges that
we've created here.

00:12:56.680 --> 00:13:01.000
So, to do that, we click on the graph, and
right-click, and select data.

00:13:01.000 --> 00:13:14.680
And then we want to change the horizontal
category axis label, and edit, so we click
on edit, and we change it from these bin
values, which are currently selected, to
these range values instead.

00:13:14.680 --> 00:13:28.680
And we just press OK, and OK again, and
you can see that it's updated our
histogram, and now we can understand what
these bars actually mean, in terms of the
ranges that they apply to.

00:13:28.680 --> 00:13:44.680
Okay, so the next thing we'll do is delete
this series label, so we just click on
series 1 and press delete, and I suppose
we want to add some axis labels to this
graph, so we can understand it even
better.

00:13:44.680 --> 00:13:52.680
So, to do that, we go to chart tools
layout in the ribbon, and then axis
titles, and we'll label the primary
horizontal axis first.

00:13:52.680 --> 00:14:07.680
We'll choose title below axis, and that
gives us axis title as the title, so we
want to select that, delete it, and
replace it with daily return range.

00:14:07.680 --> 00:14:21.680
Click off it, and then the same thing to
the vertical axis title, we'll choose
rotated title, select it, and delete, and
we'll rename it as frequency.

00:14:21.680 --> 00:14:38.680
Okay, so that's our histogram done, so we
can graphically see the frequency of daily
S&P returns that lie within these ranges
historically over the period that we've
considered.

00:14:38.680 --> 00:14:50.680
Okay, so I've already talked about how
this distribution is much like a normal
bell-shaped curve, but for those of you a
little bit more familiar with the normal
distribution,

00:14:50.680 --> 00:14:55.680
we can see that these bars don't tail off
quite as quickly as a normal distribution
would.

00:14:55.680 --> 00:15:14.680
Basically meaning the tails are slightly
fatter, that we can see visually, meaning
there's more extreme values, and we'll
come on, we'll sort of prove that in our
data as we continue, but we can already
see that this isn't quite a normal
distribution.

00:15:14.680 --> 00:15:38.680
So, we can use Excel to give us some
summary statistics of our data set, and to
do that we go to data, and data analysis,
then we're going to choose descriptive
statistics, and press ok.

00:15:38.680 --> 00:15:44.680
And our input range is our returns column
from cell H2.

00:15:44.680 --> 00:16:03.680
Control shift down, selects the whole
returns column, and we'll scroll back up
to the top of the spreadsheet, and we want
our output range to be on this
spreadsheet, on this worksheet, so our
descriptive data is on this worksheet, and
so we change the output options to output
range.

00:16:03.680 --> 00:16:23.680
And then remember to click off the output
range, and we're going to use cell T2 to
display the descriptive statistics, and
we'll need to make sure that we choose
summary statistics at the bottom to
display our descriptive statistics before
pressing ok.

00:16:23.680 --> 00:16:37.680
So this will load a number of summary
statistics of our data, so it's done it
now, we'll just adjust the column widths,
so we can see this data, see these
statistics a bit more easily.

00:16:37.680 --> 00:16:41.680
Ok, so let's interpret some of these.

00:16:41.680 --> 00:16:49.680
So the mean, this is just the average
daily return of the S&P 500 over the
historical period that we're looking at.

00:16:49.680 --> 00:16:53.680
Ok, so this is about 0.03%.

00:16:53.680 --> 00:16:59.680
Intuitively we would know it's positive,
because we know the S&P 500 is up since
1962.

00:16:59.680 --> 00:17:17.680
The median is the middle number of the
data set, so if I had a data set of 1%,
2%, and 10%, the middle of that data set
would be 2%, the middle number.

00:17:17.680 --> 00:17:19.680
So that's the median.

00:17:19.680 --> 00:17:38.680
Intuitively, by seeing the median, the
middle number is above the mean, we know
that more values lie above the mean than
below the mean, so the ones below the mean
must be more extreme.

00:17:38.680 --> 00:17:46.680
And so we can interpret this as the
negative values being more extreme than
the positive ones.

00:17:46.680 --> 00:17:48.680
Ok, so let's continue.

00:17:48.680 --> 00:17:50.680
The standard deviation is the next one.

00:17:50.680 --> 00:17:58.680
We can just interpret this one as a
measure of the dispersion of the data.

00:17:58.680 --> 00:18:11.680
Ok, so in a normal distribution, you might
know that there's roughly 68% of the
frequency of the data lies within one
standard deviation either side of the
mean.

00:18:11.680 --> 00:18:14.680
So in this case that would be 1% either
side of the mean.

00:18:14.680 --> 00:18:20.680
That's not necessarily true, in fact it
isn't true in our empirical distribution
as we'll see further on.

00:18:20.680 --> 00:18:30.680
So just bear in mind it's a measure of our
data's dispersion.

00:18:30.680 --> 00:18:35.680
So next we'll look at the kurtosis and
skewness values.

00:18:35.680 --> 00:18:42.680
These are just values that help us
understand whether our data is normally
distributed or not.

00:18:42.680 --> 00:18:54.680
So the kurtosis value is a measure of how
peaked the data is and how the tails
differ from a normal distribution.

00:18:54.680 --> 00:19:01.680
So a normal distribution in terms of how
Excel displays the kurtosis value would
have a value of 0.

00:19:01.680 --> 00:19:14.680
So this high kurtosis value basically
means that our data set, our empirical
data set, is more peaky and has higher or
fatter returns in the tails.

00:19:14.680 --> 00:19:19.680
Ok, so fatter tails in our data set.

00:19:19.680 --> 00:19:33.680
And the skewness value as a minus number
means that basically our fat tail, our
negative fat tail, is bigger than our
positive tail.

00:19:33.680 --> 00:19:42.680
Meaning that extreme negative movements
are more likely to occur than extreme
positive movements.

00:19:42.680 --> 00:19:52.680
Ok, so the next value we look at is the
range which is just the difference between
the highest daily return that's been in
our data set to the lowest daily return.

00:19:52.680 --> 00:19:57.680
And that's split up into the maximum
minimum which are the next two cells.

00:19:57.680 --> 00:20:07.680
So we can see that the lowest daily return
is minus 20% which is twice the magnitude
of the largest positive return which is
just 10%.

00:20:07.680 --> 00:20:12.680
So again that backs up our idea about
skewness.

00:20:12.680 --> 00:20:16.680
Negative extremes are of greater magnitude
than positive extremes.

00:20:16.680 --> 00:20:21.680
The sum is just the sum of our entire
returns column.

00:20:21.680 --> 00:20:24.680
Effectively it means nothing other than
that.

00:20:24.680 --> 00:20:34.680
It's certainly not the return that you
would yield if you held this asset from
1962 to 2012.

00:20:34.680 --> 00:20:42.680
And finally the count is the number of
trading days within our data set.

00:20:42.680 --> 00:20:49.680
So now we've interpreted our descriptive
statistics a little bit.

00:20:49.680 --> 00:20:59.680
Let's display the frequencies of our
ranges in our data in terms of
probabilities so we can interpret them a
bit more easily.

00:20:59.680 --> 00:21:11.680
Ok, so in column O let's head this column
with probabilities.

00:21:11.680 --> 00:21:21.680
And in O2 we type the formula, sorry in O3
we type the formula for probabilities
which is just the frequency, the number of
days within this range.

00:21:21.680 --> 00:21:31.680
So that's M3 divided by the total number
of trading days, that's the count in our
descriptive statistics which is U16.

00:21:31.680 --> 00:21:33.680
And that will give us a percentage.

00:21:33.680 --> 00:21:47.680
But before we enter this formula into this
cell we're going to press F4 which puts
these dollar signs around the denominator
of the equation when our cursor is on the
denominator.

00:21:47.680 --> 00:22:09.680
It fixes the cell that your cursor is
within and when I pressed F4 just then it
fixed the denominator which basically will
allow us to drag down the formula, copy
the formula down to the cells below and it
will just move the numerator down
appropriately but it won't change this
cell, the denominator.

00:22:09.680 --> 00:22:17.680
Ok, so if we just press enter and then
select that cell again and drag this
formula down.

00:22:17.680 --> 00:22:24.680
And while that's still selected if we
right click within that selection and
format the cells so that their percentages
are displayed as percentages.

00:22:24.680 --> 00:22:39.680
Ok, so we can see for example that 5.64%
of our data, of our daily return values
lie within minus 1.5 to minus 1.1%.

00:22:39.680 --> 00:22:43.680
Ok, so that we can interpret all these
probabilities like that.

00:22:43.680 --> 00:23:02.680
So basically for this first value that
would be 2.23% of our return values lie
lower than minus 2% so less than or equal
to minus 2%.

00:23:02.680 --> 00:23:23.680
Another way to understand and interpret
these probabilities a bit more easily is
if we display them in cumulative
probabilities which will allow us to
analyze sections of the data.

00:23:23.680 --> 00:23:38.680
So if we head column P, so column P, so
column P, cell P2 and we put cumulative
probabilities, adjust the column width
appropriately.

00:23:38.680 --> 00:23:44.680
And this time in the first cell P3 we just
type equal to O3.

00:23:44.680 --> 00:24:03.680
In cell P4 we type equal to P3 plus O4 and
then we can copy this formula down in cell
P4 and this will give us our cumulative
probability distribution.

00:24:03.680 --> 00:24:13.680
Ok, so what is meaning when we can
interpret this a bit better than our
normal probabilities column is because we
can start saying things like

00:24:13.680 --> 00:24:23.680
10.56% of the data lies with the return of
less than minus 1%.

00:24:23.680 --> 00:24:30.680
Ok, so 10.5% of the time the S&P 500
yields returns of less than minus 1%.

00:24:30.680 --> 00:24:38.680
And you can turn that on its head and say
that 90% of the time the S&P 500 yields a
return of greater than minus 1%.

00:24:38.680 --> 00:24:45.680
So you can kind of interpret these how you
like and manipulate it how you like.

00:24:45.680 --> 00:24:51.680
Ok, so let's go ahead and tidy the
spreadsheet up a little bit now.

00:24:51.680 --> 00:24:57.680
If we just tidy these values up that we've
just been looking at, we'll create borders
around these cells.

00:24:57.680 --> 00:25:14.680
So if we select cell L2 down to P12 and
then go to the home tab on the ribbon and
with this range still selected, go to the
borders drop down and select all borders.

00:25:14.680 --> 00:25:18.680
That will give borders around those cells
and we'll move the graph to the right hand
side.

00:25:18.680 --> 00:25:23.680
So let's zoom out the spreadsheet, move
the graph to the right hand side before we
continue.

00:25:23.680 --> 00:25:27.680
So our spreadsheet looks a little better.

00:25:27.680 --> 00:25:43.680
Ok, so we've basically done, considered
some basic statistics and got a basic
understanding of how our distribution
works using the tools that Excel has given
us so far.

00:25:43.680 --> 00:25:51.680
But what we can do is manipulate this data
further to get a real in-depth
understanding of what's going on.

00:25:51.680 --> 00:25:58.680
And we'll do that by filtering the returns
column and then analysing subsets of data.

00:25:58.680 --> 00:26:06.680
So before we continue, let's add three
rows to the top of the spreadsheet to
create a little bit more space.

00:26:06.680 --> 00:26:15.680
So we just click on row 1 on the left hand
side and right click, click insert, 1, 2
and 3.

00:26:15.680 --> 00:26:25.680
And then what we can do is, once we've
filtered these returns, we'll look at the
average of a subset.

00:26:25.680 --> 00:26:29.680
So first of all, let's add the filter to
the returns column.

00:26:29.680 --> 00:26:39.680
Ok, so we click on cell H4, the top of the
returns column, and go to data, the data
tab on the ribbon, and select filter.

00:26:39.680 --> 00:26:53.680
And this is applied drop down arrows to
all the columns here, A to H, which allows
us to filter and sort all these columns by
any of these date, open, high, low,
returns, whatever, any of those.

00:26:53.680 --> 00:27:01.680
So for example, we could change all this
data from newest to oldest to oldest in
terms of date.

00:27:01.680 --> 00:27:04.680
So that's turned the dates now upside
down.

00:27:04.680 --> 00:27:11.680
